 of 72.5 or more that never came back. Black's last stones then wiped every White stone off the board.", "moments": [{"turn": 8, "text": "G4 flipped the game from -4.0 to +2.0, a 6.0-point loss, while the engine wanted E4. White had been ahead, and this handed Black the lead. Black's E3 answered with a 6.1-point loss and gave it all back."}, {"turn": 36, "text": "H3 looked like a gain, taking Black's lead from +8.9 to +18.5, but it lost 9.6 points against the engine's B5. That is the bigger of two misses here, because White also had the chance to defend the left side. Black's A8 lost 8.0 in return, and White finally played B5 two moves later."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "A5 is the decisive blunder: the lead jumped from +11.1 to +74.6, a 63.5-point loss, while the engine's choice was A4. The board shows White's stones on the left and in the center with a shortage of liberties and eye space, and the extension at A5 left the group in trouble."}, {"turn": 44, "text": "After Black's A4 lost 58.1 points and brought the lead back down to +16.5, White had a way back into the game. B6 lost 58.4 points and sent the lead right back up to +74.9, while the engine preferred J7. Two huge swings in two moves showed how fragile both sides' groups were."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "H5 was the last big error, +13.3 to +72.5, a 59.2-point loss, with J4 the engine's choice. Black had just pushed in with J5 on the right edge, and White did not answer where the engine wanted. From then on Black stayed above +72, and by the final position none of White's stones were left on the board."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses the pasted board and flood-fills each group to print its stones and liberties, flagging groups with two or fewer liberties. It does no candidate-move scoring and plays no continuations, so it inspects the current position only.", "stop": false}}